WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.730
Aesop’s Fables. Part 10.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.988
The Shepherd’s Boy and the Wolf.

00:00:04.988 --> 00:00:22.102
A Shepherd's Boy was tending his flock near a village, and thought it would be great fun to hoax the villagers by pretending that a Wolf was attacking the sheep: so he shouted out, "Wolf! wolf!" and when the people came running up he laughed at them for their pains.

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:29.486
He did this more than once, and every time the villagers found they had been hoaxed, for there was no Wolf at all.

00:00:29.486 --> 00:00:42.375
At last a Wolf really did come, and the Boy cried, "Wolf! wolf!" as loud as he could: but the people were so used to hearing him call that they took no notice of his cries for help.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:48.414
And so the Wolf had it all his own way, and killed off sheep after sheep at his leisure.

00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:51.937
You cannot believe a liar even when he tells the truth.

00:00:51.937 --> 00:00:53.756
The Fox and the Goat.

00:00:53.756 --> 00:00:57.997
A Fox fell into a well and was unable to get out again.

00:00:57.997 --> 00:01:04.673
By and by a thirsty Goat came by, and seeing the Fox in the well asked him if the water was good.

00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:10.608
"Good?" said the Fox, "it's the best water I ever tasted in all my life.

00:01:10.608 --> 00:01:12.889
Come down and try it yourself."

00:01:12.889 --> 00:01:18.973
The Goat thought of nothing but the prospect of quenching his thirst, and jumped in at once.

00:01:18.973 --> 00:01:26.817
When he had had enough to drink, he looked about, like the Fox, for some way of getting out, but could find none.

00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:30.095
Presently the Fox said, "I have an idea.

00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:42.887
You stand on your hind legs, and plant your forelegs firmly against the side of the well, and then I'll climb on to your back, and, from there, by stepping on your horns, I can get out.

00:01:42.887 --> 00:01:45.692
And when I'm out, I'll help you out too."

00:01:45.692 --> 00:01:53.906
The Goat did as he was requested, and the Fox climbed on to his back and so out of the well; and then he coolly walked away.

00:01:53.906 --> 00:02:09.081
The Goat called loudly after him and reminded him of his promise to help him out: but the Fox merely turned and said, "If you had as much sense in your head as you have hair in your beard you wouldn't have got into the well without making certain that you could get out again."

00:02:09.081 --> 00:02:10.582
Look before your leap.

00:02:10.582 --> 00:02:12.635
The Fisherman and the Sprat.

00:02:12.635 --> 00:02:21.962
A Fisherman cast his net into the sea, and when he drew it up again it contained nothing but a single Sprat that begged to be put back into the water.

00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:31.158
"I'm only a little fish now," it said, "but I shall grow big one day, and then if you come and catch me again I shall be of some use to you."

00:02:31.158 --> 00:02:40.080
But the Fisherman replied, "Oh, no, I shall keep you now I've got you: if I put you back, should I ever see you again?

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.246
Not likely!"

00:02:41.246 --> 00:02:42.950
The Boasting Traveller.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:51.522
A Man once went abroad on his travels, and when he came home he had wonderful tales to tell of the things he had done in foreign countries.

00:02:51.522 --> 00:03:00.006
Among other things, he said he had taken part in a jumping-match at Rhodes, and had done a wonderful jump which no one could beat.

00:03:00.006 --> 00:03:05.571
"Just go to Rhodes and ask them," he said; "every one will tell you it's true."

00:03:05.571 --> 00:03:12.837
But one of those who were listening said, "If you can jump as well as all that, we needn't go to Rhodes to prove it.

00:03:12.837 --> 00:03:17.616
Let's just imagine this is Rhodes for a minute: and now—jump!"

00:03:17.616 --> 00:03:19.655
Deeds, not words.

00:03:19.655 --> 00:03:21.470
The Crab and his Mother.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:27.434
An Old Crab said to her son, "Why do you walk sideways like that, my son?

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:29.302
You ought to walk straight."

00:03:29.302 --> 00:03:35.506
The Young Crab replied, "Show me how, dear mother, and I'll follow your example."

00:03:35.506 --> 00:03:42.912
The Old Crab tried, but tried in vain, and then saw how foolish she had been to find fault with her child.

00:03:42.912 --> 00:03:45.425
Example is better than precept.
